Luanti, loaded as a separate module
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
builtin/luanti/vendor/ holds part of Luanti's own Lua layer, taken from
https://github.com/luanti-org/luanti at commit befadef8 (2026-09-05). What
was taken and what was left out: builtin/luanti/vendor/README.txt

  Luanti
  Copyright (C) 2010-2024 celeron55, Perttu Ahola <celeron55@gmail.com>
  and contributors (see source file comments and the version control log)

  This program is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify
  it under the terms of the GNU Lesser General Public License as published
  by the Free Software Foundation; either version 2.1 of the License, or
  (at your option) any later version.

  This program is distributed in the hope that it will be useful,
  but WITHOUT ANY WARRANTY; without even the implied warranty of
  MERCHANTABILITY or FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE.  See the
  GNU Lesser General Public License for more details.

  You should have received a copy of the GNU Lesser General Public License
  along with this program; if not, see <https://www.gnu.org/licenses/>.

  Full text: builtin/luanti/COPYING.LESSER

Luanti states that once, in the LICENSE.txt at the root of its own tree; the
Lua files themselves carry no per-file header. So the statement above, and
COPYING.LESSER beside the vendored tree, are how it travels with this copy.

One vendored file is under other terms and says so in itself:
vendor/builtin/common/serialize.lua is MIT, from appgurueu's modlib
(https://github.com/appgurueu/modlib, luon.lua).

No Luanti media is here. Luanti's textures and sounds are CC BY-SA, with
their authors listed in Luanti's own LICENSE.txt, and none of them are
copied into Buildat; a Luanti server sends a client its own.

The vendored code is not part of buildat_server and is not linked into it. A
Buildat module is compiled at run time into its own shared object and
dlopened, so the LGPL code lives in builtin/luanti's object alone, and it is
distributed as the Lua source it is. See doc/luanti_module.txt.

extensions/luanti_client is written for Buildat and is Apache-2.0. It speaks
Luanti's network protocol, and the constants in it that agree with Luanti's
-- which tile of a node faces which way, what the colour names mean -- are
the facts of that interface, which a client needs in order to talk to a
server at all.


